   "subject": "Trouble with rJava and packrat",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;m trying to use Packrat for reproducible package management across Mac laptops and Ubuntu machines, and I&#39;m having trouble with with the rJava package.<div><br></div><div>What I did:</div><div>- On the Mac, I navigated into an existing R project, opened the R CLI, and ran packrat::init(). There were no errors.</div><div>- I copied the R code and &quot;packrat/packrat.lock&quot; &quot;packrat/packrat.opts&quot; &quot;packrat/src/&quot; to the Ubuntu machine</div><div>- On the Ubuntu machine, I navigated into the R project directory, and started the R CLI</div><div><br></div><div>This fails with the following output:</div><div><div><br></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">The command failed with output:</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">* installing *source* package &#39;rJava&#39; ...</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">** package &#39;rJava&#39; successfully unpacked and MD5 sums checked</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for gcc... gcc -std=gnu99</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking whether the C compiler works... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for C compiler default output file name... a.out</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for suffix of executables...</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking whether we are cross compiling... no</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for suffix of object files... o</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking whether we are using the GNU C compiler... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking whether gcc -std=gnu99 accepts -g... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for gcc -std=gnu99 option to accept ISO C89... none needed</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking how to run the C preprocessor... gcc -std=gnu99 -E</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for grep that handles long lines and -e... /bin/grep</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for egrep... /bin/grep -E</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for ANSI C header files... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for sys/wait.h that is POSIX.1 compatible... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for sys/types.h... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for sys/stat.h... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for stdlib.h... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for string.h... yes</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">checking for memory.h.</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">In addition: Warning message:</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">In restore(restart = FALSE) :</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  The most recent snapshot was generated using R version 3.1.3</font></div></div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve ensured that java is installed on the Ubuntu machine.</div><div><br></div><div>As a workaround, I also explored installing rJava directly via <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">sudo apt-get install r-cran-rjava</font> and then setting it as an external library.</div><div><br></div><div>I can successfully run <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">library(&#39;rjava&#39;)</font> outside of the packrat project.  But when I try to add it as an external library inside of the packrat project, I see</div><div><br></div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">&gt; packrat::set_opts(external.packages = c(&quot;rJava&quot;))</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">Error in results[sapply(results, Negate(isTRUE))] :</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  invalid subscript type &#39;list&#39;</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">In addition: Warning message:</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">In symlinkExternalPackages(project = project) :</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  The following external packages could not be located:</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">- &#39;rJava</font></div></div><div><br></div><div>Further investigation reveals that rJava is installed under <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">/usr/lib/R/site-library/rJava</font> but when I look at the libPaths in the packrat project, the site-library directory isn&#39;t there:</div><div><br></div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">&gt; .libPaths()</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">[1] &quot;/srv/encrypted_fs/flatiron/code/bbirnbaum_r_packages/repo/blocks/blocks/pharma/analytics/research/Amgen_QA/packrat/lib/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/3.2.0&quot;</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">[2] &quot;/srv/encrypted_fs/flatiron/code/bbirnbaum_r_packages/repo/blocks/blocks/pharma/analytics/research/Amgen_QA/packrat/lib-ext&quot;</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">[3] &quot;/usr/lib/R/library&quot;</font></div></div><div><br></div><div>What can I do to make rJava work?  Ideally, it would just work without the external package workaround, but even getting that to work would be ok.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks!</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
